--- body ---
## Context

`erg update` downloads the binary over **`net/http`** from a hardcoded
upstream raw URL (`raw.githubusercontent.com/MinhHaDuong/git-erg/main/...`),
with `ERG_UPDATE_URL` / `.ergrc [update] url` as overrides. That `net/http`
import is the binary's **only network code and only cgo trigger** (~0.64 MB of
symbols). Two problems:

- **Unkind to forks.** The default silently pulls *upstream's* binary, not the
  fork's own — even though the command's own summary claims "Fetch and replace
  binary from origin". A fork that rebuilds its binary (via 0147 + CI) would
  have `erg update` overwrite it with upstream's.
- **It's the lone exception to the offline invariant**, and the lone reason
  the binary isn't trivially static.

## Decision

Fetch the committed binary via **`git`** — already a hard dependency of
*git*-erg — from the repo's own `origin` by default, instead of embedding an
HTTP client. Explicitly **not** `curl`: that would add a new dependency; git
is already present. Benefits:

- Fork-kind: you update from where you cloned (origin), and the "from origin"
  promise becomes true.
- Removes the last network code and the last cgo trigger → simpler static
  build, ~0.64 MB smaller.

Preserve the existing escape hatches and friendliness:
- Keep an explicit **upstream-tracking** override (e.g. `ERG_UPDATE_URL` /
  `.ergrc`) so a fork that *wants* upstream's binary still can.
- Keep "**exit 0 on fetch error**" so `erg update && erg validate` chains
  don't fail offline or in isolated environments.
- Keep the atomic write-then-rename and the post-update legacy-`Status:`
  migration hint.

## Relevant files

- `src/go/update.go` — drop `net/http`/`crypto/tls`; shell out to `git`
  (fetch the blob from origin, compare hash, atomic replace). Update
  `summaryUpdate`/`helpUpdate` so the docs match reality.
- `docs/erg-manual.md` regenerated; bootstrap binary refreshed.

## Test

- Update against a **local** git remote fixture (no network): replaces a stale
  binary, and reports "already up to date" on a hash match.
- Fetch failure (no remote / offline) exits 0 and leaves the binary untouched.
- Negative: `grep` the source / `go list` proves `net/http` is gone.

## Exit criteria

- [x] `erg update` works via `git fetch` from origin; no `net/http` import
      remains anywhere in the source.
- [x] Explicit upstream-tracking override still available and documented.
- [x] Offline-safe (exit 0 on fetch error); atomic replace preserved.
- [x] Help/summary text no longer over-claims; `make test`/`make validate`
      green; docs + bootstrap refreshed.

## Notes

- **needs-human**: changes the update/distribution mechanism (AGENTS.md §1
  auto-skip term "distribution model") — a human should weigh the
  origin-vs-upstream default and the fork story.
- Once this lands, 0146's *offline* guard tightens from "no network except
  `update`" to "**no network anywhere**" — coordinate ordering with 0146.
- Prior design history to read first (closed): 0011 (self-update from origin
  when online), 0023 (distribution/installation/update model — design), 0033
  (keep `erg update` binary-only, hand repo sync to `init`). This ticket
  revisits the *transport* (http→git), not those decisions; check it doesn't
  contradict them.
